    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make an issue of the panel’s gender composition, and it is not, by itself, a sign of bias or unfairness.** \n\nWhile it is completely natural to look for explanations when you narrowly miss out on a role—especially after investing effort across two rounds of interviews—raising the panel's gender composition without concrete evidence would likely harm your professional reputation in the civil service.\n\nHere is a breakdown of the key factors to consider and how best to proceed:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition\n* **Why panels look the way they do:** Selection panels in government ministries are usually composed based on seniority, relevant directorates, availability, and subject-matter expertise (e.g., hiring manager, peer director, HR representative, independent assessor).\n* **Significance of an all-female panel:** Just as an all-male panel does not inherently imply anti-female bias, an all-female panel does not inherently imply bias against male candidates. \n* **Procedural rules:** Some public service jurisdictions have guidelines encouraging gender-balanced panels where practicable. However, failing to achieve a mixed panel is usually an operational constraint (e.g., scheduling or panelist availability) rather than evidence of prejudice. Even if a guideline was not met, procedural irregularity does not automatically equate to unlawful discrimination.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Qualifications vs. Final Selection\n* **Shortlisting vs. Final Appointment:** Your degrees and ten years of civil service experience clearly demonstrated your competence—that is why you made the final three. However, once candidates reach the final interview stage, CV qualifications usually take a back seat to:\n  * Performance against specific competency frameworks during the interview.\n  * Direct relevance of specific experience (e.g., the successful candidate’s \"experience leading international projects\" may have closely aligned with an upcoming ministry priority).\n  * Strategic vision and team fit.\n* Having a law degree and MPP does not automatically rank a candidate above someone with an MA in political science who brought specific project leadership the ministry prioritised.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay in Feedback\n* Civil service HR and recruitment processes are notoriously slow. \n* Collating formal written feedback often requires the panel chair to review individual panel notes, draft comments aligned with competency scores, and have HR vet the document. Two weeks is a very standard turnaround time, not an indication of evasiveness or bad faith.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite follow-up on feedback:**\n   * After two weeks, it is appropriate to send a brief, professional nudge to the recruitment coordinator or panel chair.\n   * Frame your request around personal and professional development:\n     > *\"I understand the panel is busy, but I would greatly appreciate any written feedback when time permits to help me identify areas for development as I prepare for future senior opportunities.\"*\n2. **Offer an informal discussion:**\n   * Often, panel chairs are more willing and able to provide candid, actionable feedback over a 15-minute phone or video call than in formal written documentation.\n3. **Keep the focus on the criteria:**\n   * When you do receive feedback, focus strictly on the competency ratings and panel notes. If there are areas where the successful candidate scored higher (e.g., leadership under ambiguity, international stakeholder management), treat those as targets for your next career step.\n4. **Recognise your strong standing:**\n   * Reaching the final three out of what was likely a large applicant pool confirms you are performing at the senior policy adviser level. Maintaining strong relationships with the panel members could well lead to other opportunities or reserve-list openings in the near future.\n",
